## Why This Is Asked
Explores multi-model inference orchestration under streaming load, with precise resource guarantees and safe upgrades.

## Key Concepts
- Per-model batching with isolation
- GPU memory quotas per model
- Streaming windowing and batch_timeout
- SLA-driven routing policy
- Canary rollouts and automatic rollback
- Tail latency monitoring and drift alerts

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo config illustrating per-model batching and mem quotas
{
  "models": {
    "A": { "max_batch_size": 64, "batch_timeout_micros": 1000, "mem_quota_mb": 12288 },
    "B": { "max_batch_size": 128, "batch_timeout_micros": 1000, "mem_quota_mb": 8192 }
  },
  "routing": { "policy": "queue_depth" }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test drift detection and rollback criteria?
- What metrics and dashboards would you expose for operators?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design multi-model serving with tight SLAs on shared hardware, focusing on batching, isolation, and live upgrades.

## Key Concepts
- Per-model batching queues with isolation
- Resource budgeting on a single GPU
- Canary rollout and rollback criteria
- Latency/throughput validation and monitoring

## Code Example
```yaml
model_config_list {
  config {
    name: "transformer"
    batching_parameters {
      batch_timeout_micros: 1000
      max_batch_size: 16
      max_enqueued_batches: 4
      num_batch_threads: 2
    }
  }
  config {
    name: "vision"
    batching_parameters {
      batch_timeout_micros: 800
      max_batch_size: 8
      max_enqueued_batches: 6
      num_batch_threads: 2
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you detect head-of-line blocking and adjust queues automatically?
- What metrics and alert thresholds would you use for auto-rollback?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to design multi-tenant inference with dynamic schemas, deterministic preprocessing, and zero-downtime schema updates.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant-scoped schema registry with versioning
- Runtime feature alignment and type casting
- Per-tenant batching and routing to model versions
- Graceful schema hot-reload and in-flight request integrity
- gRPC path to TF Serving and error handling

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: load tenant schema and map fields to tensor order
function preprocess(input, tenantId, version){
  const schema = registry.get(tenantId, version);
  const ordered = schema.fields.map(f => input[f] ?? schema.defaults[f]);
  // cast types, handle missing, etc.
  return tf.tensor(ordered);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you monitor tail latency per-tenant and detect schema drift in this setup?
- What consistency model would you enforce for schema updates across replicas?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses designing cross-edge inference with routing, versioning, and observability under latency constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Edge-cloud inference
- TF-Lite vs SavedModel signatures
- Capability-based routing
- Zero-downtime upgrades with canary
- Telemetry and drift analysis

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo routing function (Python-like pseudocode)
def route(request, device_capability, online):
    if device_capability >= 8 and online:
        return cloud_inference(request, signature="full")
    else:
        return edge_inference(request, signature="lite")
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you simulate offline bursts and measure parity?
- How would you implement version rollback in case of drift?
